kosha minerals-varga

  -- inorganic solids with ordered atomic structure (crystalline) or disordered (amorphous).
  -- minerals are the inorganic face of the earth: rocks, sand, clay, salt, gems.
  -- what defines a mineral:
  --   naturally occurring, inorganic, solid, definite chemical composition,
  --   ordered internal structure (crystal lattice, except amorphous like glass).
  --
  -- three main bond types in minerals:
  --   ionic:    NaCl, CaCO3, CaF2 — ions in lattice. soluble in water. brittle.
  --   covalent: SiO2 (quartz), diamond — strong network. insoluble. hard.
  --   mixed:    most silicates — partial covalent Si-O with ionic metal cations.
  --
  -- silicates are the most abundant mineral class (90% of earth's crust).
  -- SiO4 tetrahedron is the fundamental unit — Si bonded to 4 O atoms.
  -- different ways of linking tetrahedra give different mineral structures:
  --   isolated:  olivine  — SiO4⁴⁻ units, Mg/Fe²⁺
  --   chains:    pyroxene — single chain
  --   sheets:    clay, mica — layered sheets (explains cleavage and softness)
  --   3D framework: quartz, feldspar — fully linked tetrahedra

